     "result_text_tail": "so breaks the runway rule (about 10 months at the lowest point). In a simple model I built from the pack's figures, the software business breaks even on cash around month 26 at today's 60% growth, with cash never below $5.4M. At 40% growth it breaks even around month 36. At 30% growth runway drops below 12 months around month 23 unless costs are cut. The deck also covers decisions to request, a 90-day plan, triggers for reconsidering quick commerce, and risks and assumptions.\n\n**Choices I made where the pack was unclear** (listed on slide 17 and in the README):\n- **The $9.5M:** it's unclear whether this includes today's $0.4M/month burn, so I show both readings. Both break the runway rule.\n- **\"Contribution\":** I took it to mean per-order profit before site fixed costs.\n- **\"Best observed\" values:** I treated them as a ceiling, since the best order size and best volume came from different sites.\n- **Rider minimum pay:** the pack gives no figure, so the +10% and +20% cost scenarios are illustrative.\n- **Software model:** costs are worked out from current recurring revenue and burn, and held flat apart from the $0.8M for four hires.\n\n**Not checked in PowerPoint:** the sandbox blocked Quick Look and there is no PowerPoint renderer here. I checked the layout with my own rough image previews and an automatic check that text fits its boxes, so it's worth a quick look in PowerPoint before the board meeting.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- Parcelbird_Now_board_deck.pptx\n- README.md"
    }
